  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "id": "berklee-music-theory-book-1-p0052",
      "source_en": "Lesson 21. Scale Degrees\n\nThe notes of the major scale can be identified by number, according to their place in the scale. Their location in the scale is called their scale degree. For example, D is the second note of the C major scale, and is called the “second scale degree,” “scale degree 2,” or just “2.” Numbering starts with the bottom note (1) to the top note (7). Then, the bottom note-the tonic-is repeated an octave higher.\n\n1 2 3 4 5 6 7 8(1)\n\nSyllable names are frequently used instead of numbers, especially for singing, because they are easier to sing than numbers.\n\nDo Re Mi Fa Sol La Ti Do",
